  "content": {
    "id": "p26-q4-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "4",
    "prompt": "Use Desmos to plot the following slope fields.\nhttps://www.desmos.com/calculator/cwkp7gveor\n\nIn each case:\n(i) identify isoclines where $y'=0$;\n(ii) identify any other obvious isoclines.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 3,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p26-q4-a",
        "type": "group",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$y'=-y^2$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [
          {
            "id": "p26-q4-a-i",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "i",
            "prompt": "",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$y=0$.",
              "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}-y^2&=0\\\\y&=0\\end{aligned}$$",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 13,
            "responseSpace": "scaffold"
          },
          {
            "id": "p26-q4-a-ii",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "ii",
            "prompt": "",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "Horizontal lines $y=k$ are isoclines.",
              "worked": "On $y=k$, $y'=-k^2$, which is constant along each horizontal line.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 15,
            "responseSpace": "scaffold"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p26-q4-b",
        "type": "group",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$y'=-\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [
          {
            "id": "p26-q4-b-i",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "i",
            "prompt": "",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "There are no zero-slope isoclines.",
              "worked": "For every $x\\ne0$, $-\\frac{1}{x^2}<0$, so $y'$ is never zero.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 15,
            "responseSpace": "scaffold"
          },
          {
            "id": "p26-q4-b-ii",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "ii",
            "prompt": "",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "Vertical lines $x=k$, $k\\ne0$, are isoclines.",
              "worked": "On $x=k$, $y'=-\\frac{1}{k^2}$, independent of $y$. Exclude $k=0$, where the differential equation is undefined.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 18,
            "responseSpace": "scaffold"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p26-q4-c",
        "type": "group",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$y'=\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{4}\\right)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [
          {
            "id": "p26-q4-c-i",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "i",
            "prompt": "",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$x=2+4n$, $n\\in\\mathbb Z$; for example, $x=\\pm2,\\pm6,\\ldots$.",
              "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{4}\\right)&=0\\\\\\frac{\\pi x}{4}&=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+n\\pi\\\\x&=2+4n\\end{aligned}$$ where $n\\in\\mathbb Z$.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 20,
            "responseSpace": "scaffold"
          },
          {
            "id": "p26-q4-c-ii",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "ii",
            "prompt": "",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "Vertical lines $x=k$ are isoclines.",
              "worked": "On $x=k$, $y'=\\cos(\\pi \\frac{k}{4})$, which is constant along the vertical line.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 15,
            "responseSpace": "scaffold"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p26-q4-d",
        "type": "group",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$y'=1-x+y$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [
          {
            "id": "p26-q4-d-i",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "i",
            "prompt": "",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$y=x-1$.",
              "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}1-x+y&=0\\\\y&=x-1\\end{aligned}$$",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 13,
            "responseSpace": "scaffold"
          },
          {
            "id": "p26-q4-d-ii",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "ii",
            "prompt": "",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "Lines $y=x+k$ are isoclines.",
              "worked": "On $y=x+k$,\n$$\\begin{aligned}y'&=1-x+(x+k)\\\\y'&=1+k.\\end{aligned}$$\nThe gradient is constant on each line.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 18,
            "responseSpace": "scaffold"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p26-q4-e",
        "type": "group",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$y'=\\dfrac{2y}{x}-y$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [
          {
            "id": "p26-q4-e-i",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "i",
            "prompt": "",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$x=2$ and $y=0$, excluding points with $x=0$.",
              "worked": "The differential equation requires $x\\ne0$.\n$$\\begin{aligned}y'&=\\frac{y(2-x)}{x}\\\\y(2-x)&=0\\end{aligned}$$\nThus the zero-slope loci are $y=0$ and $x=2$, within the domain.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 21,
            "responseSpace": "scaffold"
          },
          {
            "id": "p26-q4-e-ii",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "ii",
            "prompt": "",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$y=0$ is also an obvious isocline; it is already included in (i).",
              "worked": "On $y=0$, $y'=0$ for all $x\\ne0$. This is the horizontal zero-slope isocline.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 15,
            "responseSpace": "scaffold"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p26-q4-f",
        "type": "group",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "$y'=\\dfrac{2x}{y+1}-x$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [
          {
            "id": "p26-q4-f-i",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "i",
            "prompt": "",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$x=0$ and $y=1$, excluding points with $y=-1$.",
              "worked": "The differential equation requires $y\\ne-1$.\n$$\\begin{aligned}y'&=\\frac{x(1-y)}{y+1}\\\\x(1-y)&=0\\end{aligned}$$\nThus the zero-slope loci are $x=0$ and $y=1$, within the domain.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 21,
            "responseSpace": "scaffold"
          },
          {
            "id": "p26-q4-f-ii",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "ii",
            "prompt": "",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "No other obvious straight-line isoclines.",
              "worked": "Apart from $x=0$ and $y=1$, horizontal and vertical lines do not give constant gradients. Other isoclines are curved: setting the gradient to a nonzero constant $k$ gives\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x(1-y)}{y+1}&=k\\\\x-xy&=ky+k\\\\y&=\\frac{x-k}{x+k}.\\end{aligned}$$\nHere $x\\ne-k$ and points with $y=-1$ are excluded.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 21,
            "responseSpace": "scaffold"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
